  "result": "All four tests pass.\n\n**What I did:** Added `editor/buffer.py` with a `TextBuffer` class backed by an undo stack and a redo stack of small command objects. Each command records a position, the affected text, and whether it was an insert or a delete. Undo pops a command and applies its inverse, then pushes it onto the redo stack. Redo does the reverse. Any new edit clears the redo stack. Both edit methods validate bounds before touching the buffer, so a rejected edit leaves the text and history untouched.\n\n**Test result:**\n\n```\n4 passed in 0.04s\n```\n\n**Approach chosen:** I picked Approach A, the command pattern, because memory grows with the size of the edits rather than with buffer size times history length, and the LIFO stack discipline guarantees each inverse is applied against exactly the buffer state it expects, so it is just as simple to get right as snapshots for this interface.",
